WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
[ YouTube Learning ]

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.780
How much money do you really need

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.980
to start shooting videos at home?

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.680
Zero!

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:09.840
You heard me right!

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.080
You may have heard about fancy cameras and

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.660
fancy lighting, and great equipment,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.540
but you don’t need ANY of that

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.260
to START shooting videos at home

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.020
The only thing you really need

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.600
is the determination to start.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.600
[ Don't Memorise intro music ]

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.160
Remember that it’s more important to have great content

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.800
than to have a huge expensive production set.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.940
Let’s first talk about the simplest setup
possible!

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.840
To start shooting videos at home,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.520
you just need your smartphone.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.840
Because nowadays, all smartphones come with

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.280
good quality cameras.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:44.880
But is that it?

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.100
Let me give you just 4 tips

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.060
that will help you create great videos!

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.720
First, good lighting!

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.740
And you don’t need fancy lights for that.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.380
The best lighting out there

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.740
is natural light or sunlight.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.140
Also, remember that if there is one light source,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.920
then it should be in the front of your face.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.180
It shouldn’t be behind you.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.700
How do we know if the lighting is good or
not?

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.200
This, for instance, looks good,

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.380
and this, not so good.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:17.940
Also, if you are using a board,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.200
especially a whiteboard,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:20.960
make sure there are no reflections

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:21.920
seen on the board.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.280
To avoid this,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.720
you could just place the light diagonally across

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.740
and not straight towards the board.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.760
Second, good sound quality.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.840
Make sure that there’s no background noise

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.080
when you are shooting videos.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.780
Turn off your TV, close your windows

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.860
and try your best to eliminate

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.540
all external sources of sound.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.100
Another common problem when it comes to sound

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.340
is ECHO.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.640
The best way to avoid the echo

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.840
is to make sure you’re not in a completely empty room.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.940
Echo occurs mainly due to the reflection of sound.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:53.780
How can you reduce the echo?

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.800
You could have carpeted floors,

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.120
you could fill the room with furnishings,

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.100
and you could also use thick curtains

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.680
to cover the walls and windows.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.060
If you want even better sound quality,

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.100
then you could possibly invest some money

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.980
in buying a good external mic.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.840
Let me give you an example.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.800
This is how it sounds with an external phone-mic,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.700
and this is how it sounds without an in-built phone-mic.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.160
Third, good framing.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.160
Framing is all the things your audience can see

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.240
in the frame of your video.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.500
That not only includes you,

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:26.600
but also your background.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.360
Don’t be too close to the camera

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.620
or too far away from it.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.440
And have a simple, clean background

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.820
without any distractions.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.960
Treat the video as if you’re taking an actual class

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:39.460
for your students in person.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.320
You’d want no distractions whatsoever, right?

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:45.300
And lastly,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.700
the Stability of your smartphone.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.220
Your smartphone needs to be very stable

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.540
when it’s recording a video.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.280
You could buy a tripod

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.320
as simple tripods are available for cheap.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.100
But if you don’t wish to invest in one,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.820
you could use books on a table,

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:00.800
and then use your laptop for stability.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.720
Now there’s a possibility that

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.460
you want to go one step further

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.520
to create better quality videos

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.100
using some advanced equipment.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.480
What I am using right now is a DSLR camera on a tripod.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.360
Now let me show you 2 pictures on the screen;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:17.540
one taken with a cellphone

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.280
and the other with a DSLR.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.480
I’m sure you noticed the difference.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.560
A better camera will give you sharper and
crisp images,

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.280
unlike your smartphone camera.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.460
You can also use a zoom lens along with it,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.580
which will help you capture zoomed-in video

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.980
without making the video look grainy.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.960
The DSLR camera also has a sensor

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.000
that is far superior to the sensor

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.540
in your smartphone camera.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:44.900
That means it can handle many more lighting situations

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.440
relative to your smartphone camera.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.200
I’m also using a good wireless mic here

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.380
which gives a crisp sound quality

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.320
and also eliminates the noise around.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.480
Here’s how it could sound with a regular
mic;

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.740
and here’s how it sounds

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.340
with the current mic I am using.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:03.340
You should note that

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:06.020
the recordings that are generated using your DSLR camera

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.900
are stored in an SD card

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.080
which is present inside the camera.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.820
These recordings can then be transferred

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.320
to your laptop or tablet using a card reader.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.020
Now some of you might be wondering...

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:19.720
what if there are some parts in your video

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:20.640
which you want to cut?

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.080
This part is called EDITING the video.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.320
You will not believe

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.680
how easy it is to do this.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.300
There are plenty of free editing tools available out there

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:30.720
that make it so easy for you

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.180
to cut parts of your video.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.440
You could also shoot many bite-sized videos

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.940
and merge them together to create a longer video.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.920
All of this and lots more can be done easily

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:41.860
using Editing tools!

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.800
I’ll share more details about editing tools

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.340
in the description of this video!

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.960
So… how can you begin?

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.360
My suggestion would be to start off using
your smartphone,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.340
and once you start uploading videos consistently,

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.960
the love you get from your audience

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.660
would automatically make you invest

00:04:58.660 --> 00:04:59.740
in better equipment.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.460
So what are you waiting for?

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.860
Shoot your first video,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.160
and share it with the world!

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:05.880
In the next video,

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.920
I will tell you how you can UPLOAD your videos

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.260
to your YouTube channel.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:10.300
See you there!

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:17.640
[ #TeachFromAnywhere ]

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.160
[ YouTube Learning ]

